<KnowledgeLayout title="Image Token Calculation: OpenAI vs Claude vs Gemini" description="A source-linked explanation of image token calculation across OpenAI, Anthropic Claude, and Google Gemini, including detail, resizing, tiles, patches, and API verification." heading="Image token calculation across OpenAI, Claude, and Gemini" eyebrow="Provider analysis · Engineering guide" datePublished="2026-08-28" dateModified="2026-08-28" section="Multimodal tokenization" keywords={['image token calculator','OpenAI image tokens','Claude image tokens','Gemini image tokens']} {toc}>
  <p class="knowledge-lead">The same JPEG can occupy different token budgets across providers—and across generations of models from one provider. The count follows the model's visual preprocessing contract, not the compressed file size.</p>
  <h2 id="why">Why image token counts differ</h2>
  <p>Vision pipelines typically transform the source image before inference. Relevant variables include width and height, aspect ratio, a requested detail or media-resolution mode, maximum resize bounds, tile or patch dimensions, per-tile budgets, base tokens, and model-specific multipliers. Consequently, “one image equals N tokens” is only valid when a provider explicitly fixes the image budget for that model and mode.</p>
  <h2 id="openai">OpenAI: detail mode and model-specific budgets</h2>
  <p>OpenAI image inputs expose <code>low</code>, <code>high</code>, and <code>auto</code> detail. Low detail uses a fixed low-resolution budget; high detail adds detailed crops after resizing. Token multipliers and base budgets vary by model, so a formula copied from an older vision model must not silently be applied to a newer one. TokenCalculator.dev selects a formula only where the model registry supports it and labels unavailable combinations.</p>
  <h2 id="claude">Claude: resized pixel area converted to tokens</h2>
  <p>Anthropic documents an approximate image-token relationship based on dimensions after any required resizing: <code>tokens ≈ width × height ÷ 750</code>. Very large images may be resized before this calculation. The estimate is useful for preflight planning, while actual API usage is authoritative.</p>
  <h2 id="gemini">Gemini: multimodal countTokens and media resolution</h2>
  <p>Google's documentation states that all modalities are tokenized and provides a <code>countTokens</code> endpoint. Current guidance also distinguishes small-image budgets, tiling for larger images, and media-resolution controls on newer Gemini models. Because behavior is model-dependent, the API endpoint is the reconciliation standard; the browser formula is a transparent planning implementation.</p>
  <h2 id="workflow">A defensible comparison workflow</h2>
  <ol><li>Keep the exact same image and record its pixel dimensions.</li><li>Select the exact model generation and detail/media-resolution mode.</li><li>Measure locally in the <a href="/">image token calculator</a>.</li><li>Call the provider token endpoint or inspect usage metadata.</li><li>Store both values with the model ID and date; do not generalize one result to another model family.</li></ol>
